Setting Up Your Environment First, you need to prepare your computer to run Hyperledger Fabric. This involves installing some tools and software.
#1. Install Prerequisites 
#We Run this command in terminal using Linux system (Ubuntu -> 24.04.2) for installs tools like curl (for downloading files), 
git (for version control), docker and docker-compose (for running the network in containers), golang (for Go programming), make 
(for building software), and jq (for handling JSON data).
sudo apt install curl git docker.io docker-compose golang make jq -y

#Enable Docker service: Docker is like a virtual machine that runs the blockchain network. 
#Enable and start the Docker service, and add your user to the Docker group so you can run Docker commands without needing sudo every time
sudo systemctl enable docker
sudo systemctl start docker
sudo usermod -aG docker $USER

#2. Install Fabric Samples with FabCar. This includes the core programs (binaries), Docker images, and sample projects to get you started. 
#Run this command from your home directory
curl -sSL https://bit.ly/2ysbOFE | bash -s -- 2.2.7 1.5.7

#This installs:
Binaries: peer, orderer, fabric-ca-client, etc.
Docker images
Clones: fabric-samples/

#Install Go (Required for Chaincode)
sudo apt update && sudo apt install golang-go -y
echo 'export PATH=$PATH:/usr/lib/go/bin' >> ~/.bashrc
source ~/.bashrc

#3. Launch the Test Network. Let’s fire up a simple test network to start with. 
#This network will have two organizations (Org1 and Org2) and an orderer (the component that organizes transactions).
#Navigate to the test network directory:
cd ~/fabric-samples-old/fabcar$ 

#Run FabCar Network 
~/fabric-samples-old/fabcar$ ./startFabric.sh

#4. Interact with JavaScript
#The output involves navigating to a JavaScript directory, installing Node.js dependencies with npm install, 
enrolling an admin identity with node enrollAdmin.js, and registering an application user with node registerUser.js for a Hyperledger Fabric application.
cd javascript
npm install  # Install dependencies
#Run node enrollAdmin.js  # Create admin identity
~/fabric-samples-old/fabcar/javascript$ node enrollAdmin.js
Wallet path: /home/gregory/fabric-samples-old/fabcar/javascript/wallet
Successfully enrolled admin user "admin" and imported it into the wallet

#Run node registerUser.js  # Register app user
~/fabric-samples-old/fabcar/javascript$ node registerUser.js
Wallet path: /home/gregory/fabric-samples-old/fabcar/javascript/wallet
Successfully registered and enrolled admin user "appUser" and imported it into the wallet


#5. Confirm Network and Chaincode Status. To check if the chaincode is installed on your peers (the nodes in Org1 and Org2)
#Check installed chaincode:
peer lifecycle chaincode queryinstalled

#Check committed chaincode:
peer lifecycle chaincode querycommitted --channelID testchannel

#6. Executing JavaScript Queries and Transactions for Asset Management (FabCar)
#The output involves running JavaScript scripts in a Hyperledger Fabric application to query all cars (node query.js), 
add a new car with ID CAR12 (node invoke.js), and verify the addition of CAR12 by querying again (node query.js)

#Run node invoke.js  # Add new car (CAR12)
